// SDL's own numbers, asked for rather than written down.
//
// **These are in the pleasant layer on purpose**, though every one of them is a `c const` and this
// file therefore carries a header clause. The split between the two modules is about what an
// *application* has to do, not about where a C artifact may sit: a program that opens a window names
// `INIT_VIDEO` and `WINDOW_RESIZABLE`, and asking it to import a module documented as "nothing here
// is meant to be called by a program" to get them was the split leaking rather than doing its job.
// Nothing in `sh.sysl.sdl3.c` reads any of these -- they were only ever for callers.
//
// **`@include` is a property of the FILE, not of the module**, so this one repeats what `c.sysl`
// used to carry. A second file of this module writing a `c const` block with no header clause of its
// own is refused by the C compiler, naming the identifier rather than the missing include -- which is
// why all of them are in one file instead of sitting beside the calls they belong to.
//
// **A hundred and eighteen of them a program never spells**, because this layer already turns them
// into variants -- `EventKind`, `SystemCursor`, `BlendMode`, `TextureAccess`, `ScaleMode`,
// `TextInputType`, `PixelFormat`, `SystemTheme`. They are here because the code above needs them,
// and a consumer reads the enum instead.
//
// Each is what the C compiler computes for SDL's own name, out of the header named below, on the
// target being built. `15 §7` calls a transcribed constant "correct on one machine" with "nothing
// checking it", and this is what it asks for instead.
//
// **It found two that were wrong.** `SDL_WINDOW_ALWAYS_ON_TOP` had been written down as `0x8000`,
// which is `SDL_WINDOW_MOUSE_RELATIVE_MODE` — so a window asking to stay above the others got the
// pointer warped and hidden instead. `SDL_EVENT_TEXT_EDITING` had been written down as `0x304`,
// which is `SDL_EVENT_KEYMAP_CHANGED` — so an IME's composition events simply never matched. Both
// compiled, linked and ran; neither was anything a sysl test could have noticed, because the number
// it would have compared against is the number that was wrong.
//
// **SDL's headers are not on the toolchain's default search path**, so a consumer passes
// `--include-path` beside the `--link-path` this package already needs. `package.hocon` declares
// that requirement, which is what turns a missing header into a sentence naming this package rather
// than a clang error naming a file nobody wrote.

module sh.sysl.sdl3

@include("SDL3/SDL.h")

// ### Subsystems (SDL_INIT_*)
c const
    INIT_AUDIO:    u32 = "SDL_INIT_AUDIO"
    INIT_VIDEO:    u32 = "SDL_INIT_VIDEO"
    INIT_JOYSTICK: u32 = "SDL_INIT_JOYSTICK"
    INIT_HAPTIC:   u32 = "SDL_INIT_HAPTIC"
